---
import type { Language } from '../playground/runtime-bridge';
type Props = { language: Language };
const { language } = Astro.props;
const literals = language === 'kindly' ? 'true, false, none' : 'True, False, None';
const operators = [
  ['Call and indexing', 'f(...), value[index]', 'Left to right'],
  ['Unary arithmetic', '+value, -value', 'Right to left'],
  ['Multiplication', '*, /, %', 'Left to right'],
  ['Addition', '+, -', 'Left to right'],
  ['Comparison and equality', '<, <=, >, >=, ==, !=', 'Left to right'],
  ['Logical negation', 'not', 'Right to left'],
  ['Logical conjunction', 'and', 'Left to right'],
  ['Logical disjunction', 'or', 'Left to right'],
];
---

<section aria-labelledby="values">
  <h2 id="values">Values and literals</h2>
  <p>
    A value can be a number, text, a boolean, a collection, or a function. Its type determines what
    you can do with it. The following forms write values directly in your program:
  </p>
  <div>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Writing a value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Number</th><td
            >Write an integer such as <code>42</code>, or a decimal such as <code>3.14</code>. Use a
            leading minus for a negative number: <code>-5</code>. Decimals have digits on both sides
            of the point.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">String</th><td
            >Put text between double quotes: <code>"Hello"</code>. A string fits on one line.
            Characters between the quotes, including backslashes, are used literally.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Boolean and null</th><td
            ><code>{literals}</code>. Booleans represent true and false. The null value represents
            the absence of a value, including the result of a function that returns without a value.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">List</th><td
            >Put values in square brackets, separated by commas: <code>[1, "two", 3]</code>. A list
            keeps its values in order and can be changed.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Dictionary</th><td
            >Put key-value pairs in braces: <code>{'{"name": "Ada", "score": 90}'}</code>. Separate
            each key and value with a colon. Keys may be strings, numbers, booleans, or null.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Deque</th><td
            >Call <code>deque()</code> to create an empty deque, or <code>deque([1, 2])</code> to start
            with values. A deque allows values to be added or removed at either end.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Function</th><td
            >A function is also a value. You can store it in a variable, pass it to another
            function, or return it from a function.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lists and dictionaries can contain other collections. In a dictionary, a later entry with the
    same key replaces the earlier value.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="expressions">
  <h2 id="expressions">Expressions</h2>
  <p>
    An expression produces a value. For example, <code>2 + 3 * 4</code> produces <code>14</code>:
    multiplication is performed before addition. Parentheses let you choose a different order, so <code
      >(2 + 3) * 4</code
    > produces <code>20</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This table lists operators from highest to lowest precedence. The last column gives the grouping
    order when operators have the same precedence.
  </p>
  <div>
    <table>
      <thead
        ><tr
          ><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col">Operators</th><th scope="col">Grouping</th
          ></tr
        ></thead
      >
      <tbody
        >{
          operators.map(([name, syntax, associativity]) => (
            <tr>
              <>
                <th scope="row">{name}</th>
                <td>
                  <code>{syntax}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{associativity}</td>
              </>
            </tr>
          ))
        }</tbody
      >
    </table>
  </div>
  {
    language === 'simple' && (
      <p>
        Assignment (<code>=</code>) has the lowest precedence and groups right to left.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <h3>Arithmetic and comparison</h3>
  <p>
    Arithmetic works on integers and decimals. Division produces a decimal result: <code>5 / 2</code
    > is <code>2.5</code>. The remainder operator, <code>%</code>, gives the remainder after
    division: <code>10 % 3</code> is <code>1</code>. Its result has the same sign as the first
    operand; the second operand must be nonzero.
  </p>
  <p>
    Addition also joins strings: <code>"Hello, " + "Ada"</code> produces <code>"Hello, Ada"</code>.
    Use two strings when joining text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <code>==</code> to test whether values are equal, and <code>!=</code> to test whether they differ.
    The operators <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, and <code>&gt;=</code> compare
    numbers. To test a range, combine comparisons: <code>score &gt;= 50 and score &lt; 80</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>Boolean expressions</h3>
  <p>
    <code>and</code> combines conditions that must both hold. <code>or</code> combines alternatives. <code
      >not</code
    > reverses a condition. For example, <code>age &gt;= 18 and active</code> checks both age and activity.
  </p>
  <p>
    A condition can use any value. Null, false, zero, empty strings, and empty collections count as
    false; other values count as true.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>and</code> evaluates its right operand only when the left operand counts as true. <code
      >or</code
    > evaluates its right operand only when the left counts as false. They return the selected operand.
    <code>not</code> returns a boolean.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="collections">
  <h2 id="collections">Indexing and mutation</h2>
  <p>
    Use square brackets to select a value: <code>items[0]</code> selects the first entry of a list or
    deque, and <code>record["name"]</code> selects a dictionary value by key.
  </p>
  <p>
    List, deque, and string indices start at zero. A negative index counts from the end, so <code
      >items[-1]</code
    > selects the last entry. The index must be an integer within the sequence's bounds. Indexing a string
    returns one character as a string.
  </p>
  <p>
    Updating an entry changes the collection. A dictionary update can add a new key. Reading a
    missing key raises an error; use <a href="/docs/standard-library#dictionaries">get</a> to supply a
    fallback value.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two variables can refer to the same collection. Changing an entry through one variable is
    visible through the other. The <a href="/docs/standard-library#collections">copy</a> function creates
    a separate outer collection; collections nested inside it remain shared. Strings keep their contents
    unchanged; create a new string to produce different text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <a href="/docs/standard-library#slicing">slice</a> to select a range. For example, <code
      >slice([10, 20, 30, 40], 1, 3)</code
    > returns <code>[20, 30]</code>. The start is included and the stop is excluded.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="execution">
  <h2 id="execution">Running a program</h2>
  <p>
    Open the editor, enter a program, and select <strong>Send</strong>. The reply shows values
    written by <code>print</code>. Each run starts with fresh variables and the <a
      href="/docs/standard-library">standard library</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the program has a syntax error, correct the highlighted location before running it again. An
    error during execution stops the program at that point. Output already printed remains in the
    reply, along with the error location and function call stack.
  </p>
</section>
